Modification of the existing Bathtub Spillway into Bathtub with Rubber Gate Weir. The crest elevation is at elevation 152.0 m, the rubber crest weir elevation is at elevation 154.0 m, and the length of the rubber crest weir is 323.00 m; 

Construction of the Proposed Auxiliary Spillway with broad-crested weir with an elevation of 152.0 m and a rubber weir crest with an elevation of 154.0 m. The channel shape is trapezoidal;

Improvement of irrigation areas, construction of main farm ditches, repair/improvement of roads, installation of turnouts, and post-harvest facilities are included in the project. Capability building for Irrigators’ Associations and acquisition of Right of Way are also components of the said project.

The NGDC Project aims to provide colocation facilities, operations and maintenance, support for the servers, cloud infrastructure and hosting services to partner government agencies. Currently, the NGDC is structured into five (5) components: 
           (a) E-Government Data Centers (Design and Build); 
           (b) E-Government Colocation Services;
           (c) E-Government Cloud Hosting Services;
           (d) E-Government Regional Data Center Services; and
           (e) Project Management Operations 

In support of the continuing development of the NGDC, additional resources are being procured, secured and maintained to ensure continuous provision of services and address increasing demands from various agencies.

Significantly, the NGDC Project supports the Department’s goal of building the physical infrastructure to interconnect government agencies. It serves as the launching point for many government services such as cloud computing, web hosting, server colocation, and other operations.

A number of agencies operate their own data centers or outsource their data center needs. The current trend in governments, however, is geared toward data center consolidation, not only to cut back on costs, but to optimize ICT resources and operations, and address data security concerns. The NGDC makes faster data exchange and collaboration among government agencies. It provides centralized servers and colocation and storage facilities in order to deliver faster, secure, reliable, and transparent government online services for the people.

Special Provisions in the GAA for the DFA Building Fund Program

Acquisition or construction of new properties abroad and in the Philippines for chanceries and residences, as well as office space for consular office through direct purchase or lease-purchase agreement;

Renovation of deteriorating government-owned consular offices, chanceries and residences of the Philippine Foreign Service;

Purchase of furniture, fixtures and equipment for chanceries, residence and consular offices;

Long-term lease of chanceries, residences, and consular offices when the terms and conditions are favorable and advantageous to the government;

Payment for the services of a high technical consultant (HTC) relative to the renovation, redevelopment and construction of chanceries, residences and consular offices; and

Payment of rental of a temporary office space for use by Philippine Foreign Services Posts during construction or major renovation of chanceries, residences and consular offices.

